neg_score, inf indicates impossible. But we must be careful: when comparing, we need to treat inf as worse. So we can initialize all with (float('inf'), 0, ()) and then set dp[0][0] = (0,0,()). Then iterate i from 1 to n:\n   for w in range(budget+1):\n       # option not take\n       best = dp[i-1][w]\n       # option take if lengths[i-1] <= w\n       if lengths[i-1] <= w:\n           prev = dp[i-1][w - lengths[i-1]]\n           if prev[0] != float('inf'): # reachable\n               new_neg_score = prev[0] - scores[i-1]  # because prev is neg_score, subtract score makes it more negative (better)\n               new_tokens = prev[1] + lengths[i-1]\n               new_indices = prev[2] + (i-1,) # tuple concatenation\n               candidate = (new_neg_score, new_tokens, new_indices)\n               if candidate < best:  # lexicographic comparison\n                   best = candidate\n       dp[i][w] = best\n- Then after loop, find best among dp[n][w] for w=0..budget. We just need the minimal tuple (neg_score, tokens, indices). Since neg_score is negative of score, smaller means higher score. But note that dp[n][w] might all be (inf,...) for unreachable. We should take the one with smallest tuple. The minimal tuple will be the best.\n\nBut we also need to handle the case where no items selected (empty selection). That might be valid? Possibly budget allowed but no items yield best? But if all scores are positive, empty selection has score 0. That's fine.\n\nThen output: {\"selected_indices\": list(indices), \"used_tokens\": tokens, \"summed_score\": -neg_score}.\n\nEdge cases: floats may have rounding issues? We'll treat them as given.\n\nLexicographic comparison of indices: Since tuples compare elementwise, and indices are in increasing order, it's fine.\n\nNow, produce the Python code as a string. Ensure it is valid JSON string with escaped characters.\n\nWe'll write solve function inside. Also include validation? The problem says \"Validate every required field b